#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:7
#: b7b3b9c675e94889a240b95b1485e282
msgid "ESC Signalling Issues"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:9
#: cbf4b3fa1cb742bf8151898a63281654
msgid "This page discusses several potential issues with ESC signaling that can result in unreliable operation or even crashes on takeoff. These primarily occur with larger QuadPlanes that have long runs to their ESCS from power and the autopilot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:13
#: 084a4de2c4d14a4b9585a39e80395cd9
msgid "Inadequate Signaling Level"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:15
#: b83e6eb8738d4edaa84bc56ba1492c62
msgid "Most autopilots use TTL/CMOS compatible signaling on their outputs, but usually only at 3.3V levels. This can ultimately result in poor signaling waveforms or levels at the ESC, resulting in unreliable operation. Contributing factors to that are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:17
#: b25e26fa5cd5492e9027ea18d4891a07
msgid "High impedance (either resistive or reactive) wiring runs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:18
#: bf632dd7193843fc983fab08618aa600
msgid "ESC input loading is too heavy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:20
#: 12f43a21bf2547599caede39450ef623
msgid "For large, high-value vehicles, its always good to check the ESC signal levels for each ESC with a scope to assure clean signaling. This is best done with a ground runup to at least 70% throttle, either physically securing the vehicle or mounting its props reversed temporarily."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:22
#: 696e25e62c954162bcb30212eb89a379
msgid "Below is an example scope trace for two ESC signals for ESCs presenting too high an input load or too much wiring resistance. As shown, the second ESC's analog level is about 3V, which is a little marginal, but the first ESC trace is only about 2V and is too low for reliable operation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:27
#: e46d9aef8dca4bd488b1fc54a19980ce
msgid "It is also possible to see poor rise and fall times, but with adequate voltage levels due to high reactance in the connection. This will also affect timing and performance and should be corrected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:30
#: 4279bb9830b242fda4c3e7b77e136908
msgid "Possible Solutions:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:32
#: e708046b07394176a48c8ba2105c0066
msgid "lower wiring impedance with larger wiring or shorter runs"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:33
#: 7c660e3f7ca445298b374de0b99e0593
msgid "use ESCs that do not present such high input loading"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:34
#: d2227de477734df5984bc567eea9d174
msgid "add buffers to autopilot outputs to provide 5v signaling and higher drive currents"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:35
#: 1c5be509d5524c7bb71b9ea77b86a418
msgid "some autopilots have the option of increasing their signal levels from 3.3V to 5V using the :ref:`BRD_PWM_VOLT_SEL<BRD_PWM_VOLT_SEL>` = 1. See the following list of autopilots that support this (if you can see this parameter in your GCS parameter list, then your autopilot has this feature):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-esc-issues.rst:38
#: e34ea4572f534965a50da3e2e14db0c1
msgid "Autopilots with :ref:`BRD_PWM_VOLT_SEL<BRD_PWM_VOLT_SEL>`"
